<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Java Sea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-sea">Java Sea</a> was a naval battle fought on 27 February 1942 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, in which a Japanese fleet defeated an Allied squadron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, British, Australian and American ships. The defeat opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> to Japanese invasion. It brought a rapid end to Dutch rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">East Indies</a>, the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-java-sea.jpg" alt="Battle of the Java Sea" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haguro-1942.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japanese-invasion-of-the-dutch-east-indies">Japanese invasion of the Dutch East Indies</a> advanced rapidly, with troop convoys screened by destroyers and cruisers and covered by aircraft from captured bases. Opposing them was a small force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, American, British and Australian warships, many of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> vintage. On 19 February 1942 Japanese air raids rendered Darwin useless as a supply and naval base, and an Allied attack off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> that night resulted in the Battle of Badung Strait. On 27 February Rear Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karel-doorman">Karel Doorman</a> sailed from Surabaya to intercept a Japanese convoy approaching from the Makassar Strait. His force, drawn from several navies, had little experience of each other's methods, and a language barrier separated the Dutch-speaking Doorman from the English-speaking ships.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Allied force comprised two heavy cruisers, three light cruisers and nine destroyers. It was sighted at 15:48 and the first shots were fired at about 16:00. Gunfire on both sides missed at first, and the Japanese fired 43 torpedoes at long range without effect. The heavy cruiser Haguro then crippled HMS Exeter by gunfire and sank the destroyer HNLMS Kortenaer with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a>, and the destroyer HMS Electra was sunk in a destroyer action. After almost two hours Doorman had not come near the convoy and withdrew towards Surabaya.</p><p>After dark his ships turned back. A floatplane from Haguro had been shadowing them, and Haguro and Nachi, low on gun ammunition, attacked with torpedoes shortly before midnight. One struck the light cruiser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, which blew apart and sank in about fifteen minutes with only 19 survivors. Another hit the flagship De Ruyter, which sank over several hours, killing Doorman. The destroyer HMS Jupiter was lost to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> mine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In the following days the cruisers USS Houston and HMAS Perth were sunk in the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait">Sunda Strait</a> on the night of 28 February, and on 1 March the damaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter">Exeter</a> and the destroyers Encounter and Pope were sunk in a second battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-sea">Java Sea</a>.</p><p>The Allied fleet was dissolved, 50 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ships were scuttled at Surabaya to prevent capture, and the Dutch surface fleet was practically eradicated from Asian waters. The four American destroyers that had survived the battle escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fremantle">Fremantle</a>. ABDA forces surrendered on 9 March. The wrecks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, De Ruyter, Electra, Kortenaer, Exeter and Encounter were found between 2002 and 2007, and by 2017 all eight ships sunk in the two battles had been reduced to remnants or removed by illegal salvage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Java Sea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colony’s defences were never built for a major war: when Japan attacked in 1942, an Allied squadron was destroyed in the <b>Battle of the Java Sea</b> on 27 February, and the KNIL surrendered at Kalijati on 8 March 1942.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Allied defeat at the <b>Battle of the Java Sea</b> on 27 February 1942 opened Java to invasion, and the Dutch forces capitulated on 8 March, ending in a matter of weeks a presence dating back almost three and a half centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the East Indies the Dutch fleet under Karel Doorman was destroyed in the <b>Battle of the Java Sea</b> in February 1942 and the colony surrendered to Japan in March.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Battle of the Java Sea</b>, the Dutch forces surrendered at Kalijati on 8 March 1942.</blockquote><a href="/state/japanese-occupation-of-the-dutch-east-indies">Japanese occupation of the Dutch East Indies</a></li></ul></section>
